[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Array-Contains and Array-Contains-Any Queries in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building modern applications with Firebase Firestore, managing relational or multi-valued data efficiently is crucial. Firestore provides powerful query capabilities for array fields, allowing developers to query documents that contain specific elements within an array without needing complex subcollections."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Array Data Types in Firestore"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into queries, it is important to understand how arrays are stored. An array in Firestore is a basic data type that can hold a list of values, such as strings, numbers, or maps. For example, a user document might contain an array of tags, roles, or interests."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"{
  "username": "johndoe",
  "interests": ["coding", "hiking", "photography"]
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the array-contains Operator"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The array-contains query operator allows you to find documents where an array field contains a specific value. This is ideal for one-to-many relationships where you want to check if a single item exists within the array."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import { collection, query, where, getDocs } from "firebase/firestore";

const usersRef = collection(db, "users");
const q = query(usersRef, where("interests", "array-contains", "hiking"));

const querySnapshot = await getDocs(q);
querySnapshot.forEach((doc) => {
  console.log(doc.id, " => ", doc.data());
});"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the array-contains-any Operator"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you need to query for documents where an array field contains any of multiple specified values, use array-contains-any. This functions like an OR operator for array elements, matching a document if it contains at least one of the items in your search list."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import { collection, query, where, getDocs } from "firebase/firestore";

const usersRef = collection(db, "users");
const q = query(usersRef, where("interests", "array-contains-any", ["hiking", "cooking"]));

const querySnapshot = await getDocs(q);
querySnapshot.forEach((doc) => {
  console.log(doc.id, " => ", doc.data());
});"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Limitations"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit for array-contains-any: You can pass up to 10 comparison values in a single array-contains-any clause."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Index Requirements: Firestore automatically indexes array fields for array-contains and array-contains-any queries, but complex multi-field queries may require composite indexes."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Array Size: Avoid storing excessively large arrays in a single document, as document size is limited to 1MB."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging array-contains and array-contains-any, you can build flexible, high-performance search features in your Firestore applications with minimal database complexity."}]
